    topic: 'earth_space',
    year_level: 'year_10',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    diagram: {"kind":"data_table","title":"Data from an Antarctic ice core (rounded)","columns":["Time (thousand years ago)","CO₂ (ppm)","Temperature compared with today (°C)"],"rows":[["20",190,"−8"],["50",210,"−5"],["125",285,"+2"],["140",195,"−7"],["240",255,"−1"]]},
    question_text: "The table shows data from an Antarctic ice core. A student concludes: 'This proves that changes in CO₂ caused every change in temperature in the past.'\nWhy is this conclusion too strong?",
    options: ["The data show that there is no link at all between CO₂ and temperature.","Ice cores can record past temperatures but cannot record past CO₂ levels.","The table shows that temperature fell whenever CO₂ rose.","The data show CO₂ and temperature rose and fell together, but a correlation alone does not show which caused the other."],
